<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>7.6.5: Practice</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01838</md:content-id>
    <md:title>7.6.5: Practice</md:title>
    <md:uuid>811d8581-236f-4450-9e6a-e37ab3f6c805</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section class="practice" id="sect-00001">
      <para id="para-00001">Complete the following questions to practice the skills you have learned in this lesson.</para>

        <exercise id="_9e8e1ffb-b4d8-4207-835d-5b316853f320" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00001">
            <para id="para-00002">The height of a diver above the water is given by <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>h</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>t</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>5</m:mn><m:msup><m:mi>t</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>10</m:mn><m:mi>t</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>, where <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>t</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> is time measured in seconds and <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>h</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>t</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math> is measured in meters.<emphasis>Select two</emphasis> statements that are true about the situation.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00001">
            <para id="para-00003">Correct! The diver begins 3 meters above the water. The function has 1 zero that makes sense in this situation.</para>
            <para id="para-00004">Try again. Revisit activity 7.6.2.</para>
            <para id="para-00005">The correct answers are: The diver begins 3 meters above the water. The function has 1 zero that makes sense in this situation.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_726e7253-2340-4293-993b-3e75356a6cf8" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00002">

            <para id="para-00006">The height of a baseball, in feet, is modeled by the function <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>h</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> given by the equation <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>h</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>t</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>60</m:mn><m:mi>t</m:mi><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>16</m:mn><m:msup><m:mi>t</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math>. The graph of the function is shown.</para>
            <para id="para-00007">About when does the baseball reach its maximum height? Round to the nearest second.</para>
            
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00002">
            <para id="para-00008">Correct! The baseball reaches its maximum height just before 2 seconds.</para>
            <para id="para-00009">Try again. Revisit activities 7.6.3.</para>
            <para id="para-00010">The correct answer is: The baseball reaches its maximum height just before 2 seconds.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="d537365d-160b-45d7-a5fa-9a5be01e2443" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00003">
            
            <para id="para-00011">About how high is the maximum height of the baseball? Round to the nearest ten.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00003">
            <para id="para-00012">Correct! The maximum height of the baseball is about 60 feet.</para>
            <para id="para-00013">Try again. Revisit activities 7.6.3.</para>
            <para id="para-00014">The correct answer is: The maximum height of the baseball is about 60 feet.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_320c2962-b632-48a9-b394-d020c4943d1a" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00004">
            
            <para id="para-00015">When does the baseball hit the ground? Round to the nearest second.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00004">
            <para id="para-00016">Correct! The baseball hits the ground less than 4 seconds after the baseball was hit or thrown.</para>
            <para id="para-00017">Try again. Revisit activities 7.6.3.</para>
            <para id="para-00018">The correct answer is: The baseball hits the ground less than 4 seconds after the baseball was hit or thrown.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_6e732bac-e06a-4304-920f-7e278feda78f" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00005">
            <para id="para-00019">The following is a graph of the functions. Use the graph to answer the questions a  –  c.</para>
            <para id="para-00020">Two rocks are launched straight up in the air. The height of Rock A is given by the function <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>, where <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>t</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>4</m:mn><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>30</m:mn><m:mi>t</m:mi><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>16</m:mn><m:msup><m:mi>t</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math>. The height of Rock B is given by <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>g</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>, where <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>g</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>t</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>5</m:mn><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>20</m:mn><m:mi>t</m:mi><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>16</m:mn><m:msup><m:mi>t</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math>. In both functions, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>t</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> is time measured in seconds and height is measured in feet.</para>
            <para id="para-00021">
                      <media alt="Insert Alt Text">
                  <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_08_08_10_01.jpg" width="300"/>
                </media>
              
          </para>
            <para id="para-00022">Which graph is the height of Rock A, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>t</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>4</m:mn><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>30</m:mn><m:mi>t</m:mi><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>16</m:mn><m:msup><m:mi>t</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math>?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00005">
            <para id="para-00023">Correct! The red graph represents Rock A.</para>
            <para id="para-00024">Try again. Revisit activities 7.6.2 and 7.6.3.</para>
            <para id="para-00025">The correct answer is: The red graph represents Rock A.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_32df0bb3-df79-459f-8973-810157436d3a" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00006">
            <para id="para-00026">Which graph is the height of Rock B, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>g</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>t</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>5</m:mn><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>20</m:mn><m:mi>t</m:mi><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>16</m:mn><m:msup><m:mi>t</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math>?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00006">
            <para id="para-00027">Correct! The green graph represents Rock B.</para>
            <para id="para-00028">Try again. Revisit activities 7.6.2 and 7.6.3.</para>
            <para id="para-00029">The correct answer is: The green graph represents Rock B.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="dbbc2916-59c6-466b-aa61-9630b1d5039b" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00007">
            <para id="para-00030">Determine which rock hits the ground first.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00007">
            <para id="para-00031">Correct! Rock B hits the ground first. The graph for Rock B (<m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>g</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>t</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>5</m:mn><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>20</m:mn><m:mi>t</m:mi><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>16</m:mn><m:msup><m:mi>t</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math> crosses the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-axis at about 1.5, which means it hits the ground about 1.5 seconds after launch. The graph for Rock A (<m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>t</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>4</m:mn><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>30</m:mn><m:mi>t</m:mi><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>16</m:mn><m:msup><m:mi>t</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math> crosses the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-axis at about 2, which means it hits the ground about 2 seconds after launch.</para>
            <para id="para-00032">Try again. Revisit activities 7.6.2 – 7.6.4.</para>
            <para id="para-00033">The correct answer is: Rock B hits the ground first. The graph for Rock B <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>g</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>t</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>5</m:mn><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>20</m:mn><m:mi>t</m:mi><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>16</m:mn><m:msup><m:mi>t</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math> crosses the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-axis at about 1.5, which means it hits the ground about 1.5 seconds after launch. The graph for Rock A <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>t</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>4</m:mn><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>30</m:mn><m:mi>t</m:mi><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>16</m:mn><m:msup><m:mi>t</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math> crosses the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>axis at about 2, which means it hits the ground about 2 seconds after launch.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="d0a5702a-7551-4600-8d0a-1d3f520bf58a" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00008">
            <para id="para-00034">Object A: <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>5</m:mn><m:msup><m:mi>t</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>25</m:mn><m:mi>t</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>50</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para>
            <para id="para-00035">Each expression represents an object’s distance from the ground, in meters, as a function of time, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>t</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>, in seconds.</para>
            <para id="para-00036">Object B: <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>5</m:mn><m:msup><m:mi>t</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>50</m:mn><m:mi>t</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>25</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para>
            <para id="para-00037">Which object was launched with the greater vertical speed?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00008">
            <para id="para-00038">Correct! Object B was launched with the greater vertical speed.</para>
            <para id="para-00039">Try again. Revisit activities 7.6.2 – 7.6.4.</para>
            <para id="para-00040">The correct answer is: Object B was launched with the greater vertical speed.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_985503e9-0ee8-451d-9cec-4cd776dfdcb0" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00009">
            <para id="para-00041">Which object was launched from the greater height?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00009">
            <para id="para-00042">Correct! Object A was launched from the greater height.</para>
            <para id="para-00043">Try again. Revisit activities 7.6.2 – 7.6.4.</para>
            <para id="para-00044">The correct answer is: Object A was launched from the greater height.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>

    </section>
  </content></document>
